this is a story about people it's about  the first VidCon to take place in Europe  it's a story that could be about  traveling to apps the dam with its  two-wheeled way of life and beautiful  canals or about videos or about famous  youtubers but to me it's a story about  people thousands of us gathered together  this weekend united by an idea the idea  of community irrespective of what they  were interested in people felt they  belonged to a global family that laughed  cried and learns together all through  videos they watched on the Internet  through YouTube I've been a part of this  community for over ten years growing up  watching channels on YouTube grow from  people with cameras in their bedrooms to  huge global enterprises with Titanic  social media stars it's been an  ever-present important part of my life a  source of entertainment information and  escape it was actually through YouTube  that I met my housemate and friends Dan  and it was with Dan that I traveled to  Amsterdam  we spent the two full days at the  convention meeting with like-minded fans  of channels engaging in inflatable  warfare and going to panels and shows  one thing that's always existed in media  of all kinds is the divide between  people that create and people that  consume Hollywood stars are idolized and  viewed as separates from the public in  the same way that classical composers  and authors were but YouTube is  different to me YouTube has always been  the platform that truly democratized  media creation anyone with access to a  camera which means anyone with access to  a smartphone so basically anyone can  upload content that can be seen by  anyone in the world there are no  barriers to distribution if what you  make is entertaining or educational or  just plain good people will watch it and  what people choose to make is often a  reflection of who they are people like  Charlie McDonald and the green brothers  and Hannah ha uploaded videos to YouTube  of them being themselves talking about  what they found interesting and  important and doing so in a way that  people found engaging YouTube was a way  of taking the interesting passionate  engaging people of the world and making  them accessible to everyone like letting  that kid that everyone hopes they can  sit next to in class sit next to  everyone at once the divide between  video creator and consumer was eroded by  the fact that anyone can and often does  create content while consuming that made  by  others but also by the fact that the  very reason someone becomes well known  on the platform is because they're  engaging and relatable to their audience  but by virtue of being popular a divide  does get thrown up there's a distinction  made between us the viewing audience and  them those making the most popular  content it's inevitable when a platform  becomes as popular as YouTube not  everyone has millions of people watching  what they make  but at events like VidCon this gets  reinforced by panels that have huge  audiences sat in seats and a select few  people on the stage the panels and  sessions that we went to were a variety  of genres and sizes from an amazing  workshop on how to edit a scishow  episode to panel on science featuring my  friends Sally and Chinni and a huge  shows that have the entire convention in  the audience all of these were amazing  and it was a weird cognitive dissonance  to see people that you used to see on a  laptop in real life now for the past  seven years I've also been creating  videos and I've had some moderate  success with it and because of this  together with the generosity of my  friend Sally LePage Thank You Sally I  got to spend a large part of VidCon  hanging out with those youtubers invited  to give talks at the conference  backstage I was a little bit  trepidatious about this to be honest  they say you should never meet your  heroes and I was worried I might meet  some of the people who've been a big  part of my YouTube life for so long and  they turn out to be massive douches with  huge egos  but I really shouldn't have worried  because every youtuber that I hang out  with and I was lucky to meet a lot of  them was super lovely charming and  generally a normal person they were just  people with a passion for something be  that science entertaining music or  whatever the whole reason they became  famous on the platform was because they  were relatable people without an ego the  kind of person you just want to hang out  with which reminded me of someone well  several people these guys the audience  the rest of us all the people I chatted  to VidCon and I was lucky to chat to a  lot of them was super lovely they were  people with a passion for something he  felt part of a community and came to  VidCon to celebrate that and to meet  their favorite creators the people I met  on the convention floor some of whom I'm  lucky enough to have watched my videos  were just the same as the people I met  backstage VidCon did several things for  me that I'd like to thank Hank and John  for firstly I got a kick-ass new profile  picture so thanks for that guys but  VidCon showed me that there really is no  difference between the people that make  some of the most popular video content  in the world and the people that watch  it that passion that geekiness that  friendliness is common to everyone in  the community regardless of how popular  what they make is and concurrently  there's no barrier to becoming one of  those creators in a service like YouTube  even in the crowded marketplace that we  have today if you make good stuff in a  genuine engaging way people will watch  any barriers to that are ones that you  create for yourself VidCon was an  incredibly affirming experience it  reinforced my belief that YouTube has  changed the way that media interacts  with our lives and how meritocratic  media has become it gave me fresh  motivation for making my own content and  new perspective on the people that I  watch so thank you Hank and John but  also thank you for something else  because on top of all the new  perspective and the creators I met and  the new friends I made VidCon gave me a  fantastic weekend with one of my best  mates all made possible through you and  through YouTube said cheers guys
 

